-- title:
--   Lefebvre's variations, No. 222 — on a theme by Burau: the kernel of the Burau representation of B_4 lies in the commutator subgroup
-- statement:
--   Let $B_4$ be the braid group on four strands and $\rho_4\colon B_4\to GL_4(\mathbb{Z}[t,t^{-1}])$ the unreduced Burau representation. If $x\in B_4$ satisfies $\rho_4(x)=1$, then $x$ lies in the commutator subgroup:
--   $$\ker\rho_4\;\subseteq\;[B_4,B_4].$$
--
--   Equivalently, every braid in the kernel of the Burau representation has exponent sum $0$. If $\rho_4$ is faithful, the kernel is trivial and the statement holds; whether $\rho_4$ is faithful is a well-known open problem (it is faithful for $n\le3$ and unfaithful for $n\ge5$).
--
--   **Formalization Note** `BraidsLinksMCG.ArtinBraidGroup 4` is the presented group on the generators `σ₁, σ₂, σ₃` with Artin's relations and `BurauFaithful.burauRep 4` the platform's unreduced Burau representation into `GL (Fin 4) (LaurentPolynomial ℤ)`. The conclusion is membership in Mathlib's `commutator (ArtinBraidGroup 4)` (the subgroup generated by all commutators).
--
--   **Why this is a variation.** Partial result towards the open problem BurauFaithful.burau_faithful_four (faithfulness of the Burau representation of B_4): any braid in the kernel must have exponent sum 0, so the kernel is contained in the commutator subgroup. It follows formally from the parent (a trivial kernel is contained in any subgroup) and uses the determinant -t of the Burau matrices.
--
--   **Attribution.** This statement belongs to *Lefebvre's variations*, a series proposed by Erwan Lefebvre: each variation plays an open problem of the platform at its first non-trivial size, so that it can be settled and then reused to attack the open problem itself.
-- source:
--   Parent: BurauFaithful.burau_faithful_four (open on the platform: injectivity of burauRep 4). The statement is a necessary condition for that injectivity (a trivial kernel is contained in the commutator subgroup), so it follows formally from the parent.
--   Source: W. Burau, 'Ueber Zopfgruppen und gleichsinnig verdrillte Verkettungen', Abh. Math. Sem. Univ. Hamburg 11 (1935) (cited from memory). The proof is elementary: det(rho(sigma_i)) = -t (checked in the platform's definitions, dot_burau_self), the exponent-sum map B_n -> Z has kernel the commutator subgroup because all Artin generators are conjugate.

import Definitions.Def_BurauFaithful_UnreducedBurau

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace LefebvreVariations

/-- The kernel of the unreduced Burau representation of `B₄` lies in the commutator subgroup: the
determinant of `burauRep n (σᵢ)` is `-t`, so a braid in the kernel has exponent sum `0`. This is a
necessary condition for `BurauFaithful.burau_faithful_four`. -/
theorem burau_four_kernel_le_commutator (x : BraidsLinksMCG.ArtinBraidGroup 4)
    (hx : BurauFaithful.burauRep 4 x = 1) :
    x ∈ commutator (BraidsLinksMCG.ArtinBraidGroup 4) := by
  sorry

end LefebvreVariations
